// fig_pose.exsc -- the bone arithmetic Figulina does, as pure Exsecutor.
//
// Ported from engine/src/kiln/kiln_pose.c, whose header explains why this
// arithmetic is worth isolating: a masked overlay blend is "code of exactly
// the kind that renders plausibly when wrong -- a mask off by one bone is an
// arm that half-follows the attack". Neither failure is distinguishable from
// an animation authoring mistake in a screenshot, so it wants a proof rather
// than a look.
//
// It is here rather than in C because the same bits have to come out on the
// VR4300 and on x86-64. Figulina already ships one core on that basis
// (gen/PROVENANCE.md, the logo rasteriser); this is the second, and the first
// that is Figulina's OWN arithmetic rather than a borrowed example.
//
// -- WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY IT CANNOT BE ------------------------------
// Three of kiln_pose.c's five functions do not port, and the reason is one
// missing primitive rather than three separate problems:
//
//   fig_quat_nlerp        normalises by 1/sqrtf(len2)
//   fig_pose_blend_masked calls fig_quat_nlerp
//   fig_quat_axis_angle   needs sin and cos
//
// Exsecutor's float surface is eleven IR opcodes -- FADD FCMP FCONST FDIV
// FEXT FMA FMUL FNEG FSUB FTOI FTRUNC -- and there is no root and no
// transcendental among them, in the IR or the prelude. A software sqrt is
// not a way around it either: kiln_pose.h's stated invariant is that a
// masked blend and an unmasked one "agree about what halfway means", and the
// unmasked one is Tiny3D's t3d_quat_nlerp calling sqrtf. An approximation
// that is merely self-consistent would break exactly the agreement this file
// exists to hold.
//
// So the two below are what a port can carry today. They are not the
// interesting two, and saying so is the point: the blocker is FSQRT, it is
// one opcode, and it is measurable rather than a matter of taste.
//
// -- CONVENTIONS ---------------------------------------------------------
// Quaternions are Tiny3D's: index 0..2 are x, y, z and index 3 is w, so
// identity is {0, 0, 0, 1}. Bones are stored DEPTH-FIRST, which is what lets
// a subtree be a run of following bones and a mask need nothing but depth.

// Hamilton product, in Tiny3D's component order. Pure arithmetic -- four
// multiplies and three add/subs per lane, no division, no root -- so this is
// bit-identical on every row by construction rather than by luck.
//
// `out` is a mutable borrow (ADR 0016) and the inputs are values, so the
// aliasing question kiln_pose.c answers with a comment ("out may alias
// either input") cannot arise here: a borrow and a value cannot be the same
// object.
publica functio fig_quat_mul(out: &mutabilis acies<f32, 4>, a: acies<f32, 4>, b: acies<f32, 4>) -> u8 {
    firma ax: f32 = a[0];
    firma ay: f32 = a[1];
    firma az: f32 = a[2];
    firma aw: f32 = a[3];
    firma bx: f32 = b[0];
    firma by: f32 = b[1];
    firma bz: f32 = b[2];
    firma bw: f32 = b[3];

    (*out)[0] = aw * bx + ax * bw + ay * bz - az * by;
    (*out)[1] = aw * by - ax * bz + ay * bw + az * bx;
    (*out)[2] = aw * bz + ax * by - ay * bx + az * bw;
    (*out)[3] = aw * bw - ax * bx - ay * by - az * bz;
    redde 0;
}

// The bits of `mask` for one bone's subtree: the bone itself, plus the run of
// following bones deeper than it. Depth-first storage is what makes that a
// run and not a search.
//
// The C takes `int n` and walks to it while only ever setting bits below 32.
// Past index 31 the loop body can no longer change `mask`, so stopping at 32
// is not a narrowing -- it is the same function with the dead tail removed,
// and it is what lets the parameter be a fixed `acies` instead of a pointer
// and a length that nothing checks.
publica functio fig_pose_subtree_mask(depth: acies<u16, 32>, n: mensura, root: mensura) -> mensura {
    si root ge n {
        redde 0;
    }
    si root ge 32 {
        redde 0;
    }

    firma d_root: u16 = depth[root];
    mutabilis mask: mensura = 0;
    mutabilis bit: mensura = 1;
    mutabilis stop: u8 = 0;

    per j in 0..32 {
        si stop eq 0 {
            si j ge root {
                si j ge n {
                    stop = 1;
                } aliter {
                    si j gt root {
                        si depth[j] le d_root {
                            stop = 1;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        si stop eq 0 {
            si j ge root {
                mask = mask aut bit;
            }
        }
        bit = bit * 2;
    }
    redde mask;
}
